data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial hypothesis: a persistent order-statistic balanced BST (e.g., Persistent Treap) could efficiently manage the transaction snapshots. The goal is to support fast reads, range queries, and snapshots, given the constraints of a limited number of open transactions (MAX_OPEN_TXNS = 4), a large number of keys (200,000), and a high transaction volume. This structure could allow concurrent access and modifications across transactions without excessive copying.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Persistent AVL Tree appears to be a viable approach due to its deterministic nature, guaranteeing logarithmic height for the key range. The maximum height of the tree remains at 26 for 200,000 keys, providing a small node footprint. Path-copying during `PUT`/`DELETE` operations generates a limited number of new nodes per commit. The memory impact of the path-copying Persistent AVL Tree will be assessed given the limited number of open transactions and potential for garbage collection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

