data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The memory footprint of the Persistent AVL Tree appears manageable; with only a few hundred thousand live `Node` objects on the heap, the young-gen GC should handle short-lived snapshots efficiently. The snapshot creation (`BEGIN`) is constant time with no allocations. Read operations involve checking a small write list and then a binary search on the snapshot, resulting in logarithmic time. Range queries involve counting keys within the snapshot using subtree size information, again with logarithmic time complexity.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `COMMIT` operation's complexity needs careful consideration; persistent AVL tree inserts/deletes are logarithmic. Even with a high transaction volume, path-copying involves a manageable number of allocations. A critical area is conflict detection as specified in R22: analyzing transaction ordering and key overlaps for potential conflicts. The impact of long-lived transactions, combined with high transaction volume, presents a potential adversarial scale case for R22.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Rereading R22 confirms the critical conflict condition: any transaction committed between T's begin and commit with overlapping keys triggers a conflict, regardless of read/range operation ordering relative to U's commit. Even reads performed after U's commit can conflict if the read key was also written in U's transaction, per R22. This applies to both small and large transactions. The read and range query operations of transaction T are checked against its snapshot as of its `BEGIN` call. The logic is, therefore, still correct.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Each committed transaction will have a monotonically increasing commit sequence number. A tree structure, such as an order-statistic tree or AVL tree, mapping each written key to its latest commit sequence number will be maintained. The goal is to design conflict detection to be logarithmic in both conflict cases by checking the `lastWriteCommitSeq`. Verification is needed to ensure maintaining only the latest `commitSeq` for each key is sufficient for conflict detection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

