data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial assessment: Optimizing the `WriteSeqTree` by pruning entries for keys no longer relevant for conflict detection appears promising. Further evaluation requires analyzing if this optimization simplifies conflict detection. Checking for conflicts for each `READ` and `RANGE` involves querying `WriteSeqTree` with logarithmic time complexity.\n\nThe `COMMIT` check's complexity seems manageable, requiring at most a logarithmic number of queries to the `WriteSeqTree` per read or range operation within the transaction's lifetime. The analysis indicates the total queries across the entire log remains computationally efficient. Verification is underway, performing a detailed, rule-by-rule check across all specified sections (R1-R27, P1-P5, Sections 4-11) for comprehensive coverage.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`L` is 1, 2, or 3, a check ensures the `log` begins with the magic bytes. If the prefix matches, it returns an empty result with a `validLength` of zero, marking the tail as torn. Otherwise, a `BAD_MAGIC` exception is thrown. If `L` is 4 or greater, a similar check for the complete magic bytes is performed; phase 1 then starts.\n\nAn empty result is verified to include empty collections for state, committed, aborted, and incomplete transactions. No field or list element should be null, as `Map.of()` and `List.of()` are used which does not permit `null` values.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Standard data structures like `HashMap` and `ArrayList` are suitable for representing state and comparing elements in order, consistent with the testing approach. Section 7.2 defines how to parse frame boundaries, starting from the offset `p = 4` to determine the validity of the WAL log.\n\nWithin the frame parsing loop, checks R5-R8 are performed: R5 handles log termination, R6 identifies torn tails, R7 validates record types, and R8 verifies payload sizes against `MAX_PAYLOAD` to ensure data integrity.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

