data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Type 10 (RANGE) record's payload validation confirms record size (`n`) is correct given key lengths.  The implementation uses a concise unsigned 32-bit integer read for txn IDs. Utilizing `int` internally saves memory, leveraging `Integer.toUnsignedLong` where 64-bit values are required. The key is to ensure unsigned comparisons where appropriate to correctly handle all valid transaction IDs.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A sentinel value of `0` in an open-addressed `int[]` hash set can efficiently represent an empty slot for transaction IDs, based on R14 which ensures transaction IDs are never zero. R14 validation confirms that zero is never a valid transaction ID. Applying this sentinel value, storing all seen transaction IDs in an `int[]` hash set eliminates the need for boxing or `Long` objects.\n\nR15 outlines constraints for key lengths and byte values for PUT, DELETE, READ, and RANGE records. Key lengths must be between 1 and 32 bytes and must contain ASCII characters in the range 0x21 to 0x7E. Validation of these constraints applies to each record type.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Verifying R15 checks for valid key lengths and ASCII character ranges for key bytes requires careful implementation for record types like PUT, DELETE, READ, and RANGE. R16 confirms that PUT values and READ values (when the \"found\" flag is set) consist of ASCII characters within a valid range. R16a validates the name field in SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK records. No overlap in order occurs during record field checks within the same record type.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R17, checking for reused transaction IDs, confirms that `seenTxns` accurately tracks valid transaction starts. The implication is that any BEGIN record encountered *after* a previous valid BEGIN with the same transaction ID triggers a `DUPLICATE_TXN` exception. This is only triggered by accepted records, as the checks occur sequentially.\n\nR18, managing the open transaction limit, establishes that `TOO_MANY_OPEN` is thrown only if `openCount` reaches `MAX_OPEN_TXNS` (4) when processing a BEGIN record. This check ensures that the system's transaction capacity is enforced correctly, preventing resource exhaustion. The logic accurately limits the active transaction count.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

