data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R17, the duplicate transaction ID check, executes before the open transaction limit check (R18). This order ensures that encountering a duplicate transaction ID throws `DUPLICATE_TXN` and not `TOO_MANY_OPEN`, aligning with requirement P4. For a `BEGIN` action (R20): add the transaction ID to `seenTxns` and initialize the transaction with empty data structures for writes, savepoints, read set, and read ranges, along with the current state root and commit sequence number. For other actions (R19), first, a check determines that `txn` is an open transaction, limiting the comparison to a small set (size 4), using an efficient linear scan. R19a enforces a write limit, throwing `TXN_TOO_LARGE` if the write count reaches 8. R21's `PUT` and `DELETE` actions add a `(key, value)` write to the transaction's write list, differentiating `PUT` writes (`value != null`) from `DELETE` writes (`value == null`).\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The SAVEPOINT action logic is being carefully examined. Each `SAVEPOINT` creates an entry in a transaction's savepoint map, tracking the write count (`position`) and record offset. Existing savepoints are updated if a savepoint with the same name already exists. The constraints on the name field (ASCII range 0x21-0x7E) are also verified. The savepoint management within the transaction structure requires careful implementation to avoid excessive allocations, considering the limited number of open transactions. Efficient storage options include lazy initialization or pooling to minimize memory footprint.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering savepoint handling, the optimization is to manage savepoints lazily, allocating the internal state only upon the first `SAVEPOINT` call. A small, initially capacity-4 list of savepoint names (`byte[] spNames`), positions (`byte[] spPos`), and offsets (`int[] spOffset`) is the most memory-efficient structure. This design avoids the risk of holding stale pooled state.\n\nThe logic for `ROLLBACK` (R19b, R23b) requires careful implementation. It involves truncating the transaction's write list to the savepoint position and removing subsequent savepoints. The rollback operation must correctly clear references to ensure no memory leaks, which requires deleting savepoints other than the savepoint to which it has been rolled back. This approach minimizes memory overhead for transactions not utilizing savepoints while efficiently handling those that do.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R23b's implications are clear; a `ROLLBACK` only affects savepoints *after* the target. The target savepoint's offset remains unchanged; subsequent rollbacks can name it. Careful testing must confirm that no memory leaks occur when removing savepoint metadata from the internal structure. R23b does not dictate the deletion of `s` (the target of the `ROLLBACK`), it just removes all savepoints with a *larger* offset than s.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

