data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Iterating through the transaction's writes in reverse order to determine the `RANGE` count, the focus shifts to filtering keys based on the last write and range boundaries, confirming that only the final writes within the specified range influence the count. A careful check will be performed to ensure this approach correctly accounts for all possible scenarios, comparing the count with the current snapshot. The absence of a write in `tx.writes` indicates the key's state based on the snapshot, which is still correct. The approach guarantees that only the final writes within range are considered.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`RANGE` processing validation confirms the initial `snapCount` correctly reflects the key count within the range from the snapshot. Transactions' writes are iterated in reverse to correctly adjust `snapCount`, only considering the final write for each key. The logic accounts for PUT and DELETE operations correctly modifying the key count. If a key is present in the snapshot and the final write is PUT, the count remains incremented. If the final write is DELETE, the count is decremented.\n\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R23e, considering range queries, reveals an optimization opportunity when `lo > hi`. Since an empty range (`lo > hi`) can never cause key conflicts as per R22, it can be skipped, simplifying conflict detection. R23e states that, when `lo > hi` \"add (lo, hi) to the transaction's read ranges.\" The key insight is that no key `K` satisfies `lo <= K <= hi` when `lo > hi`, which simplifies the check to skipping the storing of `(lo, hi)`.\n\nR22 confirms that conflict detection begins by iterating the `readSet`. For each read operation in transaction `tx`, the logic will check for conflicts.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial conflict detection implementation iterates read set keys and read ranges to check for potential conflicts, terminating early if a conflict is found. Following conflict-free transactions, writes are applied sequentially to the `currentStateRoot`. Key writes are recorded within the `writeSeqTree` with an updated sequence number if other transactions remain open. There is an explicit check to confirm `openCount > 0` before updating `writeSeqTree`. The need for concurrent updates to the writeSeqTree is being reevaluated.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

