data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"When a transaction (`tx`) commits and `openCount` is zero, its writes do not need to update `writeSeqTree`. Since no other transactions are open, no future transaction's `beginCommitSeq` can be less than `currentCommitSeq`. Future transactions will always have `beginCommitSeq` greater than or equal to `currentCommitSeq`, ensuring no conflict is possible with tx's writes, thus pruning `writeSeqTree`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"If no other transactions are open, the `writeSeqTree` can be pruned because future transactions cannot conflict. This is because a transaction's begin sequence number will always be greater than or equal to the current sequence number. Therefore, resetting `writeSeqTree` to null when the open transaction count reaches zero is mathematically sound and memory-efficient. This avoids unnecessary entries in the tree.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering scenarios with continuously open transactions (`openCount >= 1`), the `writeSeqTree`'s maximum size remains manageable due to the logarithmic search, read, range query capabilities and constant memory usage. This allows for efficient key management without excessive memory consumption, regardless of transaction duration, especially for long running transactions. Conflict detection will use the write list.\n\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `COMMIT` logic has a critical update: when a transaction commits and there are open transactions, all keys written must be added to the `writeSeqTree`, mapping the key to the current commit sequence number, so there should be no conflicts. If there are conflicts, discard writes, do not update `writeSeqTree` or `currentStateRoot`, and add the transaction ID to the `aborted` list. For `ABORT`, close the transaction, discard its writes and savepoints, and append the transaction ID to the `aborted` list.\n\nThe end-of-Phase 2 result will materialize `currentStateRoot` into a pre-sized `HashMap<String, String>`.  Return the `committed` and `aborted` transaction IDs as lists of `Long`.  Finally, add all remaining open transactions to the `incomplete` list, sorting them in ascending unsigned 32-bit integer order.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Rethinking memory usage under load: a transaction with 1,000,000 reads on absent keys could exceed 200,000 unique keys, leading to significant memory growth in the read set.  A read set (e.g. `HashSet<String>`) accumulates keys potentially not in the data store. This contradicts initial assumptions about read set size. Addressing high read counts per transaction is critical.  Consider an alternative data structure to prevent unbounded memory growth.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

