data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Analyzing `rotateRight` and `rotateLeft` AVL rotations, verifying correct node re-assignment to maintain BST property and balance factor (bf) consistency. The rotations must maintain BST ordering and update the tree structure correctly. The re-balancing logic ensures logarithmic height, critical for performance. The `makeNode` function's role in creating new nodes during rotations, maintaining immutability, needs validation.\n\nThe `balance` method logic is under detailed review, ensuring correct use of rotations (`rotateRight`, `rotateLeft`) to restore AVL tree balance. Checks include correctly calculating the balance factor (`bf = height(left) - height(right)`) and invoking the correct rotation based on height differences to restore the AVL tree's balance property. Correctly handling the re-balancing steps is required.\n\nThe analysis is focusing on validating the `makeNode` function and confirming its proper utilization to create new AVL tree nodes during tree updates (inserts and deletes). Validating node creation during rotations is critical, confirming new nodes inherit the expected fields from existing nodes while maintaining the correct structural integrity and balance factors. This is required for efficient persistent tree operations.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `balance` function for insertion and deletion scenarios is being meticulously checked. When the balance factor (`bf`) is 2, indicating a left-heavy subtree, the analysis considers both tie and heavy-right cases on the `left` node's children. If the left child is right-heavy, a left-right double rotation is applied; otherwise, a single right rotation suffices. Symmetrically, for `bf == -2`, the logic considers left-heavy and tie scenarios, applying either a single left rotation or a right-left double rotation as needed to maintain AVL balance. The goal is to verify the balancing strategy for all scenarios.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Focusing on the `put` operation within the Persistent AVL Tree, the base cases and recursive structure are under verification. If a node is null, a new node is created. Comparisons determine left or right traversal, recursively calling `put` to update the subtree. The `balance` method is called to ensure tree property maintenance after subtree updates. Importantly, if the key already exists and the value is the same, the existing node is returned, preserving the key instance. This key instance preservation prevents unnecessary node creation.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `delete` operation is under review, focusing on the scenarios where the key to delete matches a node within the AVL tree. The base cases for a null node are correctly handled, returning `null`. Key comparison guides left/right traversal, recursively calling `delete` on the appropriate subtree. If the comparison leads to no subtree change, the original node can be returned (optimized to avoid unnecessary re-balancing). In the match case (`cmp == 0`), the logic correctly handles cases where either the left or right child is null. The in-order successor search in the right subtree requires careful validation.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

