data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`IntHashSet` and `StringHashSet` design is being optimized for memory and speed. `IntHashSet`, used for tracking transaction IDs, utilizes an open-addressed `int[]` table with linear probing. `StringHashSet`, tracking transaction read keys, is lazily initialized to avoid allocation if the transaction has no reads. The design avoids unnecessary allocation.\n\nThe best strategy for `StringHashSet` is to iterate a compact list, which is faster and deterministic when checking for conflicts at commit, because this stops at first conflict. Iterating `String[] table` visits `capacity` slots which is $\\le 2 \\times \\text{size}$, also $O(\\text{size})$. Both operations are $O(\\text{size})$, and conflict detection is a boolean, independent of the order. This avoids iterating an entire hash map during conflict detection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Optimizations focus on reducing object allocations. The `RangeList` stores ranges using parallel `String` arrays (`los`, `his`), avoiding `Range` objects.  If a range duplicates the last stored range, it's skipped. The `Txn` structure uses primitive arrays (`writeKeys`, `writeValues`) for writes, and lazily initializes `readSet` and savepoint data.\n\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Okay, the transaction structure's memory footprint is being revisited with an emphasis on lazy initialization for read sets, read ranges, and savepoint data to minimize overhead. Calculations confirm that even with a large number of transactions, the overhead for array-based storage in the `Txn` structure remains manageable, due to immediate garbage collection. The design avoids pre-allocation and defers the cost until elements are actually written. The use of primitive arrays within the transaction structure significantly reduces the number of objects created, which eases garbage collection overhead.\n\nNow verifying the design of `UnsignedIntList` which extends `AbstractList<Long>` and implements `RandomAccess`, using a backing `int[]` array to minimize boxing overhead for returning `committed` and `aborted` transaction IDs. The `get(int index)` method converts the unsigned integer back to `Long` on access, while `addInt(int unsignedId)` and `size()` efficiently manage the internal array. This design provides both performance and minimal allocation. The focus is to ensure the integrity of the data structures.\n\nFinally, confirming the conversion of the `currentStateRoot` to a `Map<String, String>` at the end of phase 2. If no state exists (`currentStateRoot == null`), an empty map is returned. Otherwise, a new `HashMap` is created.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial hypothesis: an optimized traversal of the `currentStateRoot` AVL tree to construct the final `HashMap<String, String>` should be the most efficient approach, potentially leveraging a custom method for direct population. This avoids any intermediate collections or unnecessary object allocations. Given the AVL tree's guaranteed height, a recursive traversal has a bounded stack depth.\n\nA recursive function, `collectToMap(StateNode node, Map<String, String> map)`, will efficiently populate the final `HashMap`, guaranteeing logarithmic time complexity based on the AVL tree's bounded height (less than 26). The methods using the `StateNode` type (e.g., `put`, `delete`) must also be checked with a max stack depth of 26.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-efe77d06-bdbf-466f-9a3c-ec43b8275778","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795552645,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The final `HashMap` population is now using a recursive approach to directly traverse and transfer `currentStateRoot`'s key-value pairs, guaranteeing logarithmic time complexity based on the tree's height, with the stack depth bounded to at most 26. This avoids intermediate collections. The recursive approach also ensures that only the final node state is copied into the map, maintaining the properties of the data structure. `String.hashCode()` has been confirmed to be deterministic.\n\nThe integrity of `Result`, `Code`, and `WalException` has been confirmed per Section 3 specifications to ensure correct error reporting. The goal is to verify the exception handling code.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

